Aerie-Purpose: Cap the reported device memory (navigator.deviceMemory, workers, Device-Memory and Sec-CH-Device-Memory) at 8 GB on desktop only while fingerprinting protection is on for the page, so a machine with more memory looks like the common 8 GB one and the real bucket is reported when protection is off (issues #115 and #124).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.cc, third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/navigator_device_memory.cc, third_party/blink/renderer/core/loader/frame_fetch_context.cc (client hints), content/browser/client_hints/client_hints.cc (AddDeviceMemoryHeader)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Upstream clamps the value to 2..32 GB on desktop (crbug.com/454354290 raised the top
from 8 to 32) and to 1..8 GB on Android. The cached process-wide value is left at
upstream's real bucket. A new overload, GetApproximatedDeviceMemory(bool), applies the
8 GB cap for a caller that says its context is protected. The process-wide static does not
decide anything, because one process can serve a protected page and an unprotected one.

Every reader decides per context:
- navigator.deviceMemory (NavigatorDeviceMemory::ShouldCapDeviceMemory): capped when the
  HeliumNoise feature is on and the window has a Helium noise token (kHardware). The browser
  sends noise tokens only when the helium.noise.enabled pref is on and the site has no per-site
  off, so a token means "protected here", the same rule as ShouldMaskGpuInfoForContext
  (#112). A worker has no tokens yet, so it fails closed and is capped while the feature is
  on, even when protection is off, until issue #113 gives workers tokens. The decision reads
  nothing the page can set.
- Device-Memory and Sec-CH-Device-Memory on requests that the renderer builds
  (FrameFetchContext): the same helper, on the requesting window.
- The two headers that the browser adds to navigations (client_hints.cc): decided in the
  browser process for the main frame site, with ContentBrowserClient::HeliumNoiseEnabled, which
  reads the pref and the helium_noise content setting (and is false when the HeliumNoise
  feature is off). A missing browser context is capped while the feature is on.

When protection is off, globally or for the site, the real bucket is reported. There is no
farbling. A machine with less than 8 GB keeps its real bucket (the lower clamp of 2 GB is
unchanged). Starting the browser with --disable-features=HeliumNoise lifts the cap
everywhere; the Bench uses that as its negative control. A per-site setting made in a private
window stays in memory (HELIUM_NOISE does not inherit into incognito and the off-the-record
profile is never written to disk), see hardening/0011.

